# SVG Icon Inside Input Field (Right Side)

Here are several ways to achieve this:

## Solution 1: Using Relative/Absolute Positioning

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Search Input</title>
    <style>
        .search-container {
            position: relative;
            width: 300px;
        }

        .search-input {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px 40px 10px 15px; /* Right padding for icon space */
            border: 2px solid #ccc;
            border-radius: 25px;
            font-size: 16px;
            outline: none;
        }

        .search-input:focus {
            border-color: #007bff;
        }

        .search-icon {
            position: absolute;
            right: 10px;
            top: 50%;
            transform: translateY(-50%);
            background: none;
            border: none;
            cursor: pointer;
            padding: 5px;
        }

        .search-icon:hover svg {
            stroke: #007bff;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="search-container">
        <input type="text" class="search-input" id="searchInput" placeholder="Search...">
        <button class="search-icon" onclick="handleSearch()">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#666" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
                <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65"></line>
            </svg>
        </button>
    </div>

    <script>
        function handleSearch() {
            const inputValue = document.getElementById('searchInput').value;
            if (inputValue.trim()) {
                alert('Searching for: ' + inputValue);
                // Add your search logic here
                console.log('Search query:', inputValue);
            } else {
                alert('Please enter a search term');
            }
        }

        // Optional: Allow Enter key to trigger search
        document.getElementById('searchInput').addEventListener('keypress', function(e) {
            if (e.key === 'Enter') {
                handleSearch();
            }
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

## Solution 2: React Component

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import './SearchInput.css';

const SearchInput = () => {
    const [searchValue, setSearchValue] = useState('');

    const handleSearch = () => {
        if (searchValue.trim()) {
            console.log('Searching for:', searchValue);
            // Add your search logic here
        }
    };

    const handleKeyPress = (e) => {
        if (e.key === 'Enter') {
            handleSearch();
        }
    };

    return (
        <div className="search-container">
            <input
                type="text"
                className="search-input"
                placeholder="Search..."
                value={searchValue}
                onChange={(e) => setSearchValue(e.target.value)}
                onKeyPress={handleKeyPress}
            />
            <button className="search-icon" onClick={handleSearch}>
                <svg
                    xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                    width="20"
                    height="20"
                    viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                    fill="none"
                    stroke="currentColor"
                    strokeWidth="2"
                    strokeLinecap="round"
                    strokeLinejoin="round"
                >
                    <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8" />
                    <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65" />
                </svg>
            </button>
        </div>
    );
};

export default SearchInput;
```

## Solution 3: Using Flexbox

```html
<style>
    .search-wrapper {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        border: 2px solid #ccc;
        border-radius: 25px;
        padding: 5px 10px;
        width: 300px;
    }

    .search-wrapper:focus-within {
        border-color: #007bff;
    }

    .search-wrapper input {
        flex: 1;
        border: none;
        outline: none;
        padding: 8px;
        font-size: 16px;
    }

    .search-wrapper button {
        background: none;
        border: none;
        cursor: pointer;
        padding: 5px;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
    }
</style>

<div class="search-wrapper">
    <input type="text" id="searchInput" placeholder="Search...">
    <button onclick="handleSearch()">
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#666" stroke-width="2">
            <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
            <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65"></line>
        </svg>
    </button>
</div>
```

## Key Points:

| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **Positioning** | Use `position: relative` on container and `position: absolute` on icon |
| **Padding** | Add right padding to input to prevent text from going under icon |
| **Button wrapper** | Wrap SVG in `<button>` for better accessibility and click handling |
| **Cursor** | Use `cursor: pointer` to show it's clickable |
| **Enter key** | Add keypress listener for better UX |

